# ENT Tutorial - Laryngology and oncology

- **Voice physiology** (causes of hoarseness)
  - Normal speech - energy source, subglottic pressure, fluidity of vocal folds (importance of lamina propria), articulation (by lips, tongue, palate, paranasal sinuses, oral cavity)
  - Dysphoonia - i.e. hoarseness (change in quality)
  - Aphonia - lost of voice (most actue aphonia is non-organic/ psychogenic)
- **Organic dysphonia:**
  - Benign - acute laryngitis (infective), vocal nodule, vocal polyp, vocal granuloma, vocal cord palsy, benign neoplasm (recurrent respiratory papilloma, HPV type 6 and 11)
  - Intermediate - chronic laryngitis
  - Malignant - laryngeal carcinoma
- **Acute laryngyitis** - generalised edema of the glottis and supraglottis (+/- infection related Sx, e.g. fever, sore throat)
- **Vocal nodule** - localised thickening of vocal cord usually between junction of anterior and middle third and nearly always bilateral
  - Risk factor - excessive vocal use/ misuse (think occupational risk factors)
  - Loc - junction between anterior and middle third (due to area of most friction)
  - Pathophysiology - friction between focal cords (hence always bilateral)
  - Mx - vocal hygiene, speech therapy +/- excision
- **Vocal polyp** - localised edema mostly at Reinke's space (lamina propria), typically unilateral
  - Mx - requires excision (speech therapy not useful)
  - Terminology (slightly different) - polyp, cyst, Reinke's edema
  - Risk factors - prior trigger such as infection or trauma, that cannot resolve (hence requires excision)
  - Bilateral Reinke's edema - harsh voice commonly seen in chronic smokers
- **Chronic laryngitis** - pre-malignant lesion, dysplasia, leukoplakia, needs histological biopsy
  - RF - chronic smoker/ alcoholic
  - Pathology - leukoplakia, erythroplakia (more malignant potential)
- \*Vocal granuloma
  - Pathology - may be nearly always at posterior glottis
  - GERD related or laryngopharyngeal reflux
- **Vocal cord palsy** - failure of movement of vocal cords:
  - Muscle problem - infiltrative CA hypopharynx, MG
  - Cricoarytenoid joint problem - RA, intubation injury
  - Nerve problem - Nucleus ambiguis to neuromuscular junction
    - Vagal problem at any any level - L \> R because longer path
- **Recurrent respiratory papilloma** - juvenile and adult type:
  - Juvenile - acquired from mother birth tract, and treatment resistent
  - Adult - acquired sexually, more treatment sensitive (because prior)
- **Laryngeal carcinoma:**
  - Non-resolving dysphonia \> 4w or progressive dysphonia \> 2w -\> refer to ENT
  - RF - smoking related
  - Clinical classification - supraglottic, glottic, subglottic:
    - Glottic - early presentation with dysphonia (direct involvement of vocal cord)
    - Suprglotic - late presentation w/ non specific symptoms (because quite large area), typically presents w/ dysphagia, otalgia, and neck LN (more lymphatic drainage)
    - Subglottic (rare if isolated, typically from glottic, i.e. transglottic) - presents w/ dyspnoea (?stridor)
  - Ix:
    - Tissue diagnosis - microlaryngoscopy Bx under GA
    - Staging Ix - CT/MRI scan, USG neck +/- FNAC, panendoscopy (field cancerisation, with similar RF, and r/o synchronous tumour 10%)
  - Mx - dependent on early or advanced disease:
    - Early disease - single modality Tx (surgery or RT)
      - Surgery - endolaryngeal surgery (Laser, Cordectomy)
    - Advanced disease - combined modality of Tx:
      - Surgery + post-op adjuvant RT
      - Concurrent chemoRT - esp for early T3, with possible organ preservation
      - Total laryngectomy +/- neck dissection
